---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Playground from '../components/Playground.astro';
import { href } from '../data/site';
---

<Layout
  title="Concepts"
  section="Concepts"
  description="Explore a simulated ContinuumOS desktop. Edit a document, move windows, invoke typed actions and try state-preserving update concepts."
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap">
    <span class="section-label">THE DESKTOP / A DESIGN EXPLORATION</span>
    <h1>
      A workspace that changes.
      <br />
      <span class="mint">A thread that stays.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Move a window. Write something worth keeping. Change the appearance or simulate a new editor
      generation. Explore what continuity could feel like.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="page-content wrap">
    <Playground />
    <section aria-labelledby="scenarios-heading" class="scenario-section">
      <div class="scenario-section-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">THREE WAYS TO EXPLORE</span>
          <h2 id="scenarios-heading">
            Make a change.
            <br />
            Keep your place.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted readable">
          Write or select text in the editor first. Updates preserve its document, selection and
          window state. All scenarios below are simulations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-cards">
        <button class="scenario-card" data-scenario="appearance">
          <span class="index">01 / APPEARANCE</span>
          <h3>A different light.</h3>
          <p>
            Validate and apply a new theme. Keep the document, selection and window exactly where
            they were.
          </p>
          <span class="scenario-start">Run / replay appearance update ↗</span>
        </button>
        <button class="scenario-card" data-scenario="behavior">
          <span class="index lavender">02 / APP BEHAVIOR</span>
          <h3>A new generation.</h3>
          <p>
            Simulate an editor replacement that adds a word count. Carry your accepted state
            forward.
          </p>
          <span class="scenario-start">Run / replay app update ↗</span>
        </button>
        <button class="scenario-card" data-scenario="rejection">
          <span class="index copper">03 / COMPATIBILITY</span>
          <h3>A considered no.</h3>
          <p>
            Reject a candidate with an incompatible schema. Leave the previous generation and your
            work intact.
          </p>
          <span class="scenario-start">Run / replay rejected update ↗</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="simulation-timeline" aria-labelledby="simulation-title">
      <div>
        <span class="micro">SIMULATED UPDATE TRACE</span>
        <h3 id="simulation-title">A handover, step by step.</h3>
      </div>
      <ol id="simulation-stages" aria-live="polite">
        <li>Choose a scenario to inspect its illustrative events.</li>
      </ol>
      <button class="quiet-button" data-reset>
        Reset workspace & discard edits ↺
      </button>
    </section>
    <div class="two-column article-block" style="margin-top:65px">
      <div>
        <h2>
          The feeling is a concept.
          <br />
          The proof is a milestone.
        </h2>
        <p>
          These windows are browser elements. This playground has no connection to the kernel and
          cannot prove guest reload, process isolation or recovery. The visual identity remains an
          exploration. Custom Rust is provisionally selected after the hosted Slint/custom
          comparison; that engineering direction does not turn this browser concept into a guest UI.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Guest acceptance will require preserved unsaved work, selection, window state and usable
          focus alongside unchanged boot identity and uptime. A polished transition is only the
          beginning.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href={href('progress/')} style="margin-top:25px">
          See the actual gates <span>↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
